          "slug": "waacking",
          "name": "Waacking",
          "learnable": false,
          "summary": "The disco-era arm-and-posing dance born in the 1970s Los Angeles underground gay clubs.",
          "description": "Waacking was created in the early 1970s in Los Angeles' underground disco clubs by Black, Latino and Asian gay dancers, who called themselves punkers. It reached the world through Soul Train, where dancers such as Tyrone Proctor, Jeffrey Daniels and Shabba Doo performed it. It nearly disappeared in the 1980s and was brought back from the 90s on by dancers including Brian Green, Archie Burnett, Princess Lockerooo and Kumari Suraj. The dance is built on fast, powerful arm work, posing inspired by old Hollywood, and character — and it is danced to disco and funk.",
          "tips": [
            "Learn the history alongside the arms. Teachers consistently say waacking is not just arms.",
            "The spelling changed over time: \"whacking\" first, later \"waacking\" with a double a."
          ],
          "related": ["three-elements", "punking", "whack", "soul-train"]
        },
        {
          "slug": "three-elements",
          "name": "Whack, punk and pose",
          "learnable": false,
          "summary": "The three parts of the dance: the strike, the character and the held shape.",
          "description": "Teachers commonly split waacking into three elements. The whack is the arm work — strikes, lines and rolls thrown with force. Punking is the character and emotion behind the movement. The pose is the held shape that shows grace, power or attitude, inspired by old Hollywood. A good round moves between all three rather than living in the arms alone.",
          "tips": [
            "If your dance is all arms, add a pose on every phrase ending and see what changes."
          ],
          "related": ["whack", "punking", "pose"]
        },
        {
          "slug": "show-the-music",
          "name": "Show the music",
          "learnable": false,
          "summary": "The founding idea: the arms exist to show what the record is doing.",
          "description": "Bagsy traces this to the drag performers who inspired the punkers: they sold a song through body, face and hands without singing a note. In waacking the hands show the beats, the rhythm, the melody and the texture of the record — you can strike, stop, grab, throw away, place, point, punch or slice, always with intention. \"Dance to express, never to impress.\"",
          "tips": [
            "Before drilling a move, ask what in the music it is showing."
          ],
          "related": ["conductors-form", "loops-and-accents", "gestures"]
        },
        {
